>h2 Overview
>tree open Overview
Cupid OS implements these USB host controllers and class drivers:

  - USB 1.1 via UHCI (IO-port registers, BAR4)
  - USB 2.0 via EHCI (MMIO registers, BAR0)
  - Intel companion routing: EHCI claims all ports first, releases LS/FS to UHCI
  - Three class drivers: HID boot protocol, Hub, Mass Storage (BBB+SCSI)
  - HID events merged into existing PS/2 event queue (unified input stream)
  - Mass storage registers as block_device_t: usb0, usb1, ...

Source files:
  drivers/pci.h + pci.c        PCI enumeration layer
  kernel/usb/usb_hc.h          Host controller vtable
  kernel/usb/usb.h + usb.c     USB core: enumeration FSM, work queue
  kernel/usb/uhci.c            UHCI 1.1 driver
  kernel/usb/ehci.c            EHCI 2.0 driver
  kernel/usb/usb_hid.c         HID keyboard + mouse
  kernel/usb/usb_hub.c         Hub class driver
  kernel/usb/usb_msc.c         Mass storage (BBB + SCSI)
>endtree

>h2 Boot Order: EHCI Before UHCI
>tree open Boot Order
>box
EHCI must initialise before UHCI. Reversing the order creates port ownership
races that can cap high-speed devices at USB 1.1 for the boot session.
>endbox

Correct call sequence:

usb_init()
  ehci_init_all()    // first: claims all ports via CONFIGFLAG=1
  uhci_init_all()    // second: receives released LS/FS ports

Why it matters:

1) EHCI writes CONFIGFLAG=1 to claim all downstream ports from the PCI
   host controller.

2) EHCI then inspects each PORTSC.LINE_STATUS. A line state of K (LS)
   or J (FS) means a non-high-speed device is attached.

3) For such ports, EHCI sets PORTSC.PORT_OWNER=1, releasing the port to
   the companion UHCI controller.

4) If UHCI runs first, it begins driving its ports before EHCI can claim
   them. A high-speed device is then reset in full-speed mode and remains
   capped at 12 Mbit/s for that boot session.
>endtree

>h2 PCI Layer
>tree open PCI Layer
Source: drivers/pci.c + drivers/pci.h

Enumeration: pci_init() scans bus 0, devices 0-31.
  - Read vendor/device ID at config offset 0x00.
  - Skip if vendor = 0xFFFF (slot empty).
  - Check header type at offset 0x0E.
  - Bit 7 of header type = multi-function: probe all 8 functions.
  - Otherwise probe function 0 only.

BAR count by header type:
  Type 0 (general device): 6 BARs
  Type 1 (PCI bridge):     2 BARs
  Type 2 (CardBus):        0 BARs

USB class codes used by pci_find_by_class():
  class=0x0C  subclass=0x03  prog_if=0x00 -> OHCI  (not supported)
  class=0x0C  subclass=0x03  prog_if=0x10 -> UHCI
  class=0x0C  subclass=0x03  prog_if=0x20 -> EHCI
  class=0x0C  subclass=0x03  prog_if=0x30 -> xHCI  (not supported)

Bus master enable and Status bit preservation:

  PCI Status register has R/WC (read/write-1-to-clear) bits. Naive
  read-modify-write that ORs Status would clear error flags. Correct:

uint32_t v = pci_read32(bus, dev, fn, 0x04);
// Mask Status (upper 16 bits) to zero before writing; safe for R/WC
v = (v & 0x0000FFFF) | PCI_CMD_BUS_MASTER;
pci_write32(bus, dev, fn, 0x04, v);
>endtree

>h2 USB Core
>tree open USB Core
Source: kernel/usb/usb.c + kernel/usb/usb_hc.h + kernel/usb/usb.h

Host controller vtable (usb_hc_t):

typedef struct usb_hc {
  const char *name;
  void *driver_data;
  uint8_t root_speed;
  int  (*submit_sync)(usb_hc_t*, usb_transfer_t*, uint32_t timeout_ms);
  int  (*submit_interrupt)(usb_hc_t*, usb_transfer_t*,
                           usb_complete_cb_t);
  int  (*cancel_interrupt)(usb_hc_t*, usb_transfer_t*);
  int  (*port_count)(usb_hc_t*);
  int  (*port_status)(usb_hc_t*, int port, uint32_t *status);
  usb_port_reset_result_t (*port_reset)(usb_hc_t*, int port,
                                        bool must_reset);
  void (*irq_handler)(usb_hc_t*);
} usb_hc_t;

Device model (usb_device_t):
  - address:     USB address assigned during enumeration (1-127)
  - speed:       USB_SPEED_LOW / FULL / HIGH
  - class_code:  bDeviceClass from device descriptor
  - vendor_id / product_id: USB vendor and product IDs
  - hub_depth:   hub nesting depth (0 = root)
  - tt_hub_addr: transaction translator hub address (HS hubs only)
  - tt_port:     TT port on that hub
  - hc:          pointer to owning HC
  - driver_data: class driver private state
  - generation:  slot lifetime checked by queued hub work

Capacity: 32 devices global (USB_MAX_DEVICES).
Hub depth cap: 5 levels.

Serialized port reconciliation:
  usb_poll() is the public polling boundary. An atomic guard folds an
  overlapping call into the poll already in progress. One poll cycle runs
  EHCI port and interrupt work, UHCI port and interrupt work, then durable
  reconciliation.

  bool usb_port_change(hc, port)
  bool usb_hub_port_change(hub_dev, port)

  Both calls report whether the 32-entry work ring accepted the request.
  Root controllers leave the hardware change pending when the ring is full.
  A hub leaves C_PORT_CONNECTION set until the core accepts and completes
  its work.

  Work remains in the ring until it completes. A failed item rotates behind
  its peers and retries after a delay that grows from 10ms to 1 second.
  Hub work carries the parent's generation, so work from a removed or reused
  hub slot is discarded.

  A reconnect removes the old root device or hub subtree first. The core then
  owns reset, enumeration, change acknowledgement, and the final status
  reread. A state change or failed acknowledgement keeps the item queued.

Enumeration FSM (per pending port):

remove old device or subtree
port_reset(hc, port, address_quarantined)
GET_DESCRIPTOR(addr=0, len=8)      // bMaxPacketSize0
SET_ADDRESS(new_addr)
GET_DESCRIPTOR(new_addr, len=18)   // full device descriptor
GET_CONFIGURATION(len=9)           // header -> wTotalLength
GET_CONFIGURATION(len=wTotalLength)// full config tree
SET_CONFIGURATION(bConfigVal)
driver_probe_match()               // bind, retry, reject, or try next

Addresses 1 through 127 use a reusable reservation map. A failure after an
ambiguous address assignment quarantines the address until reset, disconnect,
or stale-work cleanup proves it safe to reuse.
address_quarantined sets must_reset on the controller call. A successful
handoff proves that the physical reset completed before the core releases the
reserved address.

usb_control() builds an 8-byte SETUP packet and runs a 3-phase control
transfer (SETUP -> optional DATA -> STATUS) via hc->submit_sync. Ordinary
control requests make up to five attempts with timer-backed delays. The core
probes the new address after an ambiguous SET_ADDRESS and reads the active
configuration after an ambiguous SET_CONFIGURATION.

Class-driver probe results:
  USB_PROBE_NOT_SUPPORTED  try the next driver
  USB_PROBE_BOUND          publish the binding
  USB_PROBE_RETRY          unwind and keep the port work queued
  USB_PROBE_REJECTED       keep the device present but unbound

An invalid probe result also unwinds and retries. The core does not publish a
partially initialized device.
>endtree

>h2 UHCI Driver
>tree open UHCI Driver
Source: kernel/usb/uhci.c

Register access: IO-port at BAR4 (bit 0 set in BAR value).
IO base = pci_read32(..., 0x20) & ~3.

Key registers (offset from IO base):
  0x00  USBCMD      Run/Stop, Host Reset, Global Suspend
  0x02  USBSTS      Interrupt status (write 1 to clear)
  0x04  USBINTR     Interrupt enable mask
  0x06  FRNUM       Current frame number (11-bit)
  0x08  FLBASEADD   Frame list physical base (4KB aligned)
  0x0C  SOFMOD      Start-of-frame timing
  0x10  PORTSC0     Port 0 status/control
  0x12  PORTSC1     Port 1 status/control

Data structures:
  1024-entry frame list (4KB page, one 32-bit phys pointer per slot).
  Single skeleton QH (skel_qh) inserted in every frame list slot.
  32-byte Transfer Descriptors (TDs) linked off skel_qh.

>box
kmalloc returns pointers at +12 from 16-byte-aligned blocks because of its
12-byte block header. UHCI TDs and QHs require 16-byte alignment, so the
driver aligns them manually and keeps the raw pointer for kfree().
>endbox

void *raw = kmalloc(sizeof(uhci_qh_t) + 15);
uhci_qh_t *qh = (uhci_qh_t*)(((uintptr_t)raw + 15) & ~15);
// save raw for kfree(); use qh for hardware access

Legacy SMI disable (BIOS USB ownership release):

// PCI config offset 0xC0 = LEGSUP register
// 0x8F00: bit13 disables SMI, low nibble clears pending status
pci_write16(bus, dev, fn, 0xC0, 0x8F00);

Hot-plug: UHCI has no reliable hot-plug IRQ. The serialized usb_poll() cycle
calls uhci_poll_ports(). On CONNECT_STATUS_CHANGE, the driver calls
usb_port_change(hc, port). It clears the change only after the work ring
accepts the request.

Synchronous and interrupt transfers use the controller submit lock. UHCI
stops the schedule and observes the halted state before it releases
descriptor storage. Interrupt slots carry a generation and an in-flight
claim. Cancellation waits for the matching generation's DMA and callback
work to finish.
>endtree

>h2 EHCI Driver
>tree open EHCI Driver
Source: kernel/usb/ehci.c

MMIO mapping:
  BAR0 is a memory BAR (bit 0 clear). Typical address: 0xFEBxxxxx,
  above the 128MB identity-map. Must call paging_map_mmio(bar0, 4096)
  before any register access.

Register layout:
  BAR0 + 0         Capability registers (RO)
                     CAPLENGTH (1 byte) -> offset to operational regs
                     HCIVERSION, HCSPARAMS, HCCPARAMS
  BAR0 + CAPLENGTH  Operational registers (R/W)
                     USBCMD, USBSTS, USBINTR, FRINDEX
                     CTRLDSSEGMENT, PERIODICLISTBASE
                     ASYNCLISTADDR, CONFIGFLAG, PORTSC[0..N]

BIOS handoff (USBLEGSUP):
  1. Read HCCPARAMS[15:8] for extended capability list pointer.
  2. Walk list, find CapID=0x01 (USBLEGSUP).
  3. Set OS_OWNED bit (bit 24).
  4. Poll BIOS_OWNED (bit 16) for up to 1 second.
  5. Force clear if BIOS did not release.

uint32_t leg = read_ext_cap(base, usblegsup_off);
leg |= (1 << 24);   // OS_OWNED = 1
write_ext_cap(base, usblegsup_off, leg);
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
  if (!(read_ext_cap(base, usblegsup_off) & (1<<16))) break;
  pit_sleep_ms(1);
}
// force clear BIOS_OWNED
leg = read_ext_cap(base, usblegsup_off) & ~(1<<16);
write_ext_cap(base, usblegsup_off, leg);

>box
EHCI QHs and qTDs require 32-byte alignment. The driver uses the UHCI
alignment pattern with 31 bytes of padding and a saved _raw pointer.
>endbox

void *raw = kmalloc(sizeof(ehci_qh_t) + 31);
ehci_qh_t *qh = (ehci_qh_t*)(((uintptr_t)raw + 31) & ~31);

CONFIGFLAG and companion routing:

// Claim all ports for EHCI
op->CONFIGFLAG = 1;
// A low-speed K-state port can move directly when no reset is owed.
// Reset every other connected port before selecting its owner.
for (int p = 0; p < nports; p++) {
  uint32_t ps = op->PORTSC[p];
  if (LINE_STATUS(ps) == K_STATE && !must_reset) {
    op->PORTSC[p] = ps | PORT_OWNER;
  } else {
    reset_port_and_wait_for_clear(p);
    if (!(op->PORTSC[p] & PORT_ENABLE))
      op->PORTSC[p] |= PORT_OWNER;
  }
}

J-state before reset can describe a high-speed-capable device, so it is not
proof of a full-speed attachment. EHCI verifies reset assertion and clearing,
then reads PORT_OWNER back before it reports a completed companion handoff.

Async schedule: circular doubly-linked QH list with dummy head (H=1).
Periodic schedule: 1024-entry frame list owned by the controller.
  Class-driver interrupt registrations use controller-local software slots.
  usb_poll() claims one slot generation and submits it through the
  synchronous controller path.

qTD page pointers: each qTD carries 5 x 4KB page buffer pointers,
supporting up to 20KB per transfer without chaining.

EHCI IRQ handling records changed root ports in an atomic pending bitmap.
ehci_poll_ports() clears a bit only after the serialized USB core accepts the
work. Before changing ASYNCLISTADDR or releasing a QH and qTD, EHCI disables
the asynchronous schedule and verifies its state. It halts the controller if
normal schedule revocation stalls. Failure takes the panic path instead of
freeing memory still visible to hardware.

Interrupt slots carry active and cancellation state, an in-flight claim, and
a generation. Pollers release the submit lock before DMA and callback
delivery. Successful cancellation waits for that generation to finish. A
callback cannot cancel its own slot.
>endtree

>h2 HID Driver
>tree open HID Driver
Source: kernel/usb/usb_hid.c

Claimed when: bInterfaceClass=0x03 (HID) AND bInterfaceSubClass=0x01
(Boot Interface Subclass). Both keyboard and mouse use this driver.

On probe:

// Switch to boot protocol (avoid report descriptors)
usb_control(dev, 0x21, SET_PROTOCOL, 0, iface, 0, NULL);
// Disable idle reports; send only on state change
usb_control(dev, 0x21, SET_IDLE,     0, iface, 0, NULL);

Keyboard report format (8 bytes per interrupt poll):
  Byte 0: Modifier bitmap
            bit0=Left Ctrl   bit4=Right Ctrl
            bit1=Left Shift  bit5=Right Shift
            bit2=Left Alt    bit6=Right Alt
            bit3=Left GUI    bit7=Right GUI
  Byte 1: Reserved (0x00)
  Bytes 2-7: Up to 6 simultaneous keycodes (0x00=no key)

Keyboard processing:
  Diff new report against previous to find key-down and key-up events.
  Translate each keycode through HID-usage -> PS/2-scancode table
  (~100 entries covering printable ASCII, modifiers, F1-F10, arrows,
  Backspace, Enter, Escape, Tab, Delete, Insert, Home, End, PgUp/Dn).
  Inject via keyboard_inject_scancode().

Mouse report format (3 bytes per interrupt poll):
  Byte 0: Button bitmap (bit0=left, bit1=right, bit2=middle)
  Byte 1: X displacement (signed, relative)
  Byte 2: Y displacement (signed, relative)

Mouse processing:
  Inject via mouse_inject_event(dx, dy, buttons).
  Shell and GUI window manager receive USB and PS/2 mouse events through
  the same input stream.
>endtree

>h2 Hub Driver
>tree open Hub Driver
Source: kernel/usb/usb_hub.c

Claimed when: bDeviceClass=0x09 (Hub).

Probe sequence:
  1. GET_DESCRIPTOR(HUB) -> 7+ byte hub descriptor.
  2. Read bNbrPorts (downstream port count).
  3. Read bPwrOn2PwrGood (power-on delay in 2ms units).
  4. SET_FEATURE(PORT_POWER) for each port.
  5. Wait bPwrOn2PwrGood * 2 ms.

Status-change polling:
  The driver reads the interrupt IN endpoint from the active configuration
  descriptor. Its bitmap includes bit 0 for hub status and one bit for every
  downstream port.

Per changed port:

GET_PORT_STATUS(port)             // wPortStatus + wPortChange
if C_PORT_CONNECTION set:
  usb_hub_port_change(hub_dev, port)
  if work ring is full:
    leave C_PORT_CONNECTION set

The serialized core removes the old child subtree, resets the connected
port, enumerates the current attachment, clears C_PORT_RESET and
C_PORT_CONNECTION, then rereads status and change bits. It retains the work
item if state changed or acknowledgement failed. The callback does not clear
the connection change before the core accepts ownership.

Queued hub work carries the parent device generation. An old callback cannot
act on a hub slot that has been removed and reused.

Depth cap: maximum nesting depth = 5.
  Before recursing into a new hub: check parent_depth + 1 <= 5.
  Devices beyond depth 5 are silently ignored.
  (USB 2.0 spec allows 7; Cupid OS caps at 5.)

Transaction Translator routing:
  Full-speed or low-speed devices behind a high-speed hub need TT bridging.
  Driver sets on child device:
    child->tt_hub_addr = hub_dev->addr
    child->tt_port     = port
  EHCI uses these to configure SPLIT transactions in the QH.
>endtree

>h2 Mass Storage Driver
>tree open Mass Storage Driver
Source: kernel/usb/usb_msc.c

Bulk-Only Transport (BBB) phases:

Host -> Device:  CBW  (31 bytes, OUT bulk endpoint)
                  dCBWSignature = 0x43425355 ("USBC")
                  dCBWTag       = monotonic tag
                  dCBWDataTransferLength = byte count
                  bmCBWFlags: 0x80=device->host, 0x00=host->device
                  bCBWLUN = 0
                  bCBWCBLength = SCSI CDB length
                  CBWCB = SCSI Command Descriptor Block
Host <-> Device: DATA (optional, direction from bmCBWFlags)
Device -> Host:  CSW  (13 bytes, IN bulk endpoint)
                  bCSWStatus: 0=OK, 1=fail, 2=phase error

SCSI commands implemented:
  0x12  INQUIRY           Device identification string
  0x00  TEST_UNIT_READY   Check if medium is accessible
  0x25  READ_CAPACITY(10) Block count + block size
  0x28  READ(10)          Read sector range
  0x2A  WRITE(10)         Write sector range

Block device registration:
  After READ_CAPACITY success, the driver calls blkdev_register() with:
    name         = "usb0" / "usb1" / ...
    read         = blk_read
    write        = blk_write
    sector_size  = from READ_CAPACITY response
    sector_count = from READ_CAPACITY response
    driver_data  = msc_state pointer
    release      = msc_block_release

Registry and offline lifetime:
  The registry has four sparse public slots and uses the first vacancy.
  blkdev_count() reports live entries. blkdev_index_limit() returns the
  exclusive bound for an index scan. A numeric index belongs to one
  registration lifetime and may be reused after unregister.

  blkdev_get(index) acquires a reference to the exact block_device_t. Its
  caller must later call blkdev_put(dev). The registry holds one reference
  while the device remains registered. A saturated reference count makes
  blkdev_get() return NULL without wrapping.

  The read and write callbacks stay fixed for the object's lifetime. Each
  callback takes the MSC command lock and checks the online state and USB
  device pointer before starting I/O. On disconnect, MSC marks the state
  offline under that lock, clears its USB device pointer, and calls
  blkdev_unregister(). The registry removes the public slot and drops its
  reference. Borrowed references remain safe and return an I/O failure. The
  driver restores the attached online state if unregister fails, so removal
  can retry. The final blkdev_put() calls msc_block_release() and frees the
  MSC allocation.

MBR parsing (sector 0):
  Check bytes 510-511 for 0x55AA signature.
  Parse 4 x 16-byte partition entries at offsets 0x01BE - 0x01FD:
    +0  Status (0x80 = active/bootable)
    +4  Partition type
    +8  LBA start sector
    +12 LBA sector count
  FAT16 types detected: 0x04, 0x06, 0x0E.

>box
USB mass-storage automount is not implemented.

fat16.c is a single-instance driver hardcoded to ATA. Mounting a second
FAT16 volume requires per-instance state refactoring throughout fat16.c.

USB mass storage currently exposes a raw block device only.
MBR partitions are detected and logged at enumeration time.
FAT16 file operations on USB drives are not yet available.

Remaining work: heap-allocate fat16 instance state and thread it through
every VFS call. Wire usb_msc_probe() into fat16_mount().
>endbox
>endtree

>h2 Shell Commands
>tree open Shell Commands
usb: list all enumerated devices

Cupid OS> usb
[0] addr=1  speed=HIGH  vid=8086 pid=1234  class=09 parent=root
[1] addr=2  speed=FULL  vid=045e pid=0745  class=03 parent=0
[2] addr=3  speed=FULL  vid=093a pid=2510  class=03 parent=0
[3] addr=4  speed=FULL  vid=0781 pid=5567  class=08 parent=0

Fields: device index, USB address, speed (LOW/FULL/HIGH), vendor ID,
product ID, class code, parent (root = root port, number = hub index).

usb hubs: show hub tree by depth

Cupid OS> usb hubs
depth=0  addr=1  ports=4  (root hub via EHCI)
  depth=1  addr=2  port=1  speed=FULL  (HID keyboard)
  depth=1  addr=3  port=2  speed=FULL  (HID mouse)
  depth=1  addr=4  port=3  speed=FULL  (mass storage)

usb hc: show host controller boot summary

Cupid OS> usb hc
EHCI: BAR0=0xfebf0000 ports=4 BIOS_handoff=OK
UHCI: IO=0xc080 ports=2 legacy_SMI=disabled

This output confirms that EHCI initialised before UHCI and completed the BIOS
handoff.
>endtree

>h2 QEMU Test Invocations
>tree open QEMU Test Invocations
HID keyboard and mouse:

qemu-system-i386 \
  -drive if=ide,format=raw,file=cupidos.img \
  -device piix3-usb-uhci \
  -device usb-ehci \
  -device usb-kbd \
  -device usb-mouse \
  -serial stdio

USB mass storage:

# Create test image first:
dd if=/dev/zero of=test.img bs=1M count=64
mkfs.fat -F 16 test.img

qemu-system-i386 \
  -drive if=ide,format=raw,file=cupidos.img \
  -device piix3-usb-uhci \
  -device usb-ehci \
  -blockdev driver=file,filename=test.img,node-name=ustick-file \
  -blockdev driver=raw,file=ustick-file,node-name=ustick \
  -device usb-storage,id=ustick-device,drive=ustick \
  -serial stdio

Full stack (HID + storage):

qemu-system-i386 \
  -drive if=ide,format=raw,file=cupidos.img \
  -device piix3-usb-uhci \
  -device usb-ehci \
  -device usb-kbd \
  -device usb-mouse \
  -blockdev driver=file,filename=test.img,node-name=ustick-file \
  -blockdev driver=raw,file=ustick-file,node-name=ustick \
  -device usb-storage,id=ustick-device,drive=ustick \
  -serial stdio

Persistent storage hot-plug:
  The file and raw block nodes outlive the usb-storage front end. This lets
  the same image survive repeated device removal and addition.

QEMU monitor sequence:
  device_del ustick-device
  info qtree
  device_add usb-storage,id=ustick-device,drive=ustick

  Poll info qtree until ustick-device is absent before adding it again. Read
  each monitor response through the next prompt. An Error: response means the
  hot-plug operation failed.

Note: piix3-usb-uhci emulates UHCI. OHCI (VIA/SiS real hardware) is not
emulated and not supported by Cupid OS.
>endtree

>h2 Known Limits
>tree open Known Limits
OHCI:
  VIA and SiS chipsets use OHCI, while QEMU uses UHCI. Cupid OS does not
  support real OHCI hardware.

xHCI:
  USB 3.x is outside the P4 scope and is not planned.

Isochronous transfers:
  Cupid OS does not implement isochronous scheduling, so audio class,
  webcam, and video capture devices are unsupported.

BBB only (no UAS):
  USB Attached SCSI (UAS) is a USB 3.0 protocol. Not implemented.

Flat PCI bus 0 scan:
  PCI bridges are not traversed. Devices behind bridges are invisible
  to pci_find_by_class().

Power management:
  USB suspend, resume, remote wakeup, and selective suspend are not
  implemented.

HID boot protocol only:
  No HID report descriptor parser. Devices that do not support the
  boot protocol subclass (gaming keyboards, pointing devices with
  extended reports) cannot be used.

32-device global limit:
  USB_MAX_DEVICES = 32. Large installations with many hub-attached
  devices may hit this limit.

Hub depth 5:
  USB 2.0 specification allows 7 levels of hub nesting. Cupid OS caps
  at 5 for simplicity.

Interrupt callback self-cancellation:
  A callback cannot cancel its own interrupt registration. Disconnect paths
  cancel from outside the callback and wait for the claimed generation to
  finish.

FAT16 auto-mount not wired:
  USB mass storage registers as raw block device only.
  File-level FAT16 access on USB drives is planned but not yet available.
>endtree

>rule
